    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d225">

                <pb facs="0357" n="272" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72113/950: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Thurston</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-03-29T18:00:00-06:36"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29, 1919,
                                6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 31,
                            2.28 a.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Catlin<anchor n="6" xml:id="d225-f1"/> was received day before yesterday by
                    President Cabrera who apparently is ready to enter into definite negotiations
                    for sale of Empresa Electrica.</p>
                <p>During this audience the President requested Mr. Catlin to obtain for him a loan
                    of $10,000,000 to be devoted he stated to the establishment of a national bank.
                    This measure will serve the double purpose, the President stated, of stabilizing
                    exchange and emancipating Guatemala from domination by Germans and Jews.</p>
                <p>Mr. Catlin desires to know if it is agreeable to the Department that he negotiate
                    with the President for this loan and that he submit the proposal to his
                    principals.</p>
                <p>Such a loan appears to be inevitable and it would seem desirable that reputable
                    American capitalists should negotiate it. If it is our intention to encourage
                    the supplanting of present German owned undertakings here with American
                    interests, it is almost imperative that economic conditions be remedied by some
                    such means as this.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d225-f1" n="6" xml:id="d225-f1-content">
                <p>Henry W. Catlin, of the Electric Bond &amp; Share Co., New York. See the section
                    dealing with enemy property, <ref target="#pg287">pp. 287</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
